theory.intro:
Prisms and pyramids can be built from their flat parts (faces and edges). Counting the faces, edges and vertices helps you name and construct a solid.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (3,0);
\coordinate (C) at (3,1.8);
\coordinate (D) at (0,1.8);
\coordinate (E) at (1.212,0.7);
\coordinate (F) at (4.212,0.7);
\coordinate (G) at (4.212,2.5);
\coordinate (H) at (1.212,2.5);
\fill[gray!30,opacity=0.45] (A)--(B)--(C)--(D)--cycle;
\draw (A)--(B)--(C)--(D)--cycle;
\draw (B)--(F)--(G)--(C);
\draw (D)--(H)--(G);
\draw[dashed] (A)--(E)--(F);
\draw[dashed] (E)--(H);
\draw[very thick] (A)--(B) node[midway,below,font=\small] {edge};
\fill (B) circle (0.09);
\node[right,font=\small] at (3.15,-0.15) {vertex};
\node[font=\small,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] at (0.6,1.0) {face};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.question_text:
The diagram is a net with six equal square faces. Name the model it makes and describe how to assemble it.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=12mm,y=12mm,draw=black,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw (0,0) rectangle (1,1);
\draw (1,0) rectangle (2,1);
\draw (2,0) rectangle (3,1);
\draw (3,0) rectangle (4,1);
\draw (1,1) rectangle (2,2);
\draw (1,-1) rectangle (2,0);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The diagram shows a square with one triangle attached to each side. The triangles are identical equilateral triangles, and every triangle side matches a square side. Name the model it makes, list its faces and describe how to assemble it.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=12mm,y=12mm,draw=black,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw (0,0) rectangle (2,2);
\draw (0,0)--(1,-1.7320508)--(2,0);
\draw (2,0)--(3.7320508,1)--(2,2);
\draw (2,2)--(1,3.7320508)--(0,2);
\draw (0,2)--(-1.7320508,1)--(0,0);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

These drawings show a model resting on its base. Its side view is the same as its front view. Identify the model and describe which face pieces to use to build it.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=10mm,y=10mm,draw=black,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw (0,0)--(2,0)--(1,2)--cycle;
\path (1,-0.5) node {Front view};
\draw (4,0) rectangle (6,2);
\draw (4,0)--(5,1)--(6,0);
\draw (4,2)--(5,1)--(6,2);
\path (5,-0.5) node {Top view};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

foundation.question_text:
How many straws and joiners are needed for a skeletal model of this triangular prism?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (2.6,0);
\coordinate (C) at (1.3,2);
\coordinate (D) at (1.1,0.7);
\coordinate (E) at (3.7,0.7);
\coordinate (F) at (2.4,2.7);
\draw (A)--(B)--(C)--cycle;
\draw (B)--(E)--(F)--(C);
\draw (C)--(F);
\draw[dashed] (A)--(D)--(E);
\draw[dashed] (D)--(F);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

How many straws and joiners are needed for a skeletal model of this square pyramid?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\coordinate (A) at (0,0); \coordinate (B) at (3,0); \coordinate (C) at (4,0.8); \coordinate (D) at (1,0.8); \coordinate (P) at (2,3.2);
\draw (A)--(B)--(C); \draw[dashed] (C)--(D)--(A);
\draw (P)--(A) (P)--(B) (P)--(C); \draw[dashed] (P)--(D);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Name the model and count its faces.
